WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.560
Have you wondered about those flying saucers? Then watch as Chrysler brings

00:05.560 --> 00:30.560
you The Search for the Flying Saucer, starring Jack Cawker.

00:35.560 --> 00:41.560
The Search for the Flying Saucer

01:05.560 --> 01:12.560
You're smart, ain't you?

01:12.560 --> 01:26.560
Good move. Got myself cornered.

01:26.560 --> 01:28.560
Yeah. Say, who do I see about a room?

01:28.560 --> 01:33.560
In any particular about strangers. What's your business?

01:33.560 --> 01:38.560
That might be my business. Suit yourself.

01:38.560 --> 01:43.560
Say, I don't want to make a pest of myself, but who do I see about a room?

01:43.560 --> 01:46.560
Might you be a newspaper man? I might.

01:46.560 --> 01:48.560
Come to find out about the flying saucers?

01:48.560 --> 01:51.560
Look, right now I'd like to find out about a room. Look, Mr...

01:51.560 --> 01:57.560
Crazy John. Just call me Crazy John. It's all right. Can't hurt my feelings.

01:57.560 --> 01:59.560
Nobody can no more.

01:59.560 --> 02:03.560
They've done all they can to me. They?

02:03.560 --> 02:09.560
Now you're asking questions.

02:09.560 --> 02:11.560
I'm going to have to check up on you.

02:11.560 --> 02:13.560
Don't you think I won't either?

02:13.560 --> 02:16.560
Wait a minute. What about the room?

02:16.560 --> 02:19.560
I'm sorry. I was taking a sun bath.

02:19.560 --> 02:22.560
Well, don't apologize. I'm not apologizing.

02:22.560 --> 02:26.560
Say, I just got in town and I can't seem to find a place and I can't get any dope out of the old character.

02:26.560 --> 02:29.560
Could you tell me where I could find a room?

02:29.560 --> 02:32.560
You've come to the right place. Matter of fact, the only place in town.

02:32.560 --> 02:33.560
I'm Ginny Walker.

02:33.560 --> 02:34.560
Vic Russo. Hi.

02:34.560 --> 02:37.560
Won't you sign our guest register, Mr. Walker?

02:37.560 --> 02:39.560
How long have you planned to be with us?

02:39.560 --> 02:41.560
Oh, I really don't know.

02:41.560 --> 02:44.560
Twenty-five dollars a week, including meals.

02:44.560 --> 02:46.560
Incidentally, I'm a terrible cook.

02:46.560 --> 02:48.560
Say, don't tell me you're Mother Walker.

02:48.560 --> 02:50.560
No, I'm a daughter.

02:50.560 --> 02:55.560
We bought this place about two years ago and then Mother couldn't stand it, so back she went to Chicago.

02:55.560 --> 02:57.560
What brings you to our fair city?

02:57.560 --> 02:59.560
Oh, a visit. Is that unusual?

02:59.560 --> 03:01.560
Well, it is a little bit out of the way.

03:01.560 --> 03:03.560
Are you a newspaper man?

03:03.560 --> 03:04.560
Good guess.

03:04.560 --> 03:05.560
I don't know.

03:05.560 --> 03:07.560
It's about the only transience here in town.

03:07.560 --> 03:11.560
The reporters come to unearth the deep, dark mystery of our flying saucers.

03:11.560 --> 03:13.560
You know, if you'd seen one, it might not strike you so funny.

03:13.560 --> 03:15.560
Are you kidding?

03:15.560 --> 03:19.560
Oh, look, if you're here on assignment to look into all this, I...

03:19.560 --> 03:21.560
Look, I'm sorry. It's my fault.

03:21.560 --> 03:23.560
Look, could you show me to my room? I'm a little touchy.

03:23.560 --> 03:25.560
Yes, sure.

03:28.560 --> 03:29.560
Nice?

03:29.560 --> 03:32.560
Yeah, looks fine.

03:32.560 --> 03:34.560
Come on, I'll help you unpack.

03:34.560 --> 03:36.560
Oh, that's OK. Thanks a lot.

03:36.560 --> 03:40.560
I'm a little tired. I had kind of a rough trip. I think I'll take a nap, if you don't mind.

03:40.560 --> 03:41.560
Oh, surely.

03:41.560 --> 03:46.560
Oh, if you want anything, I mean, the bathroom's upstairs in the garage.

03:46.560 --> 03:53.560
Thanks. Thanks a lot. That's swell.

03:59.560 --> 04:01.560
Says he's a reporter, eh?

04:01.560 --> 04:03.560
So I'll have to check on him.

04:03.560 --> 04:06.560
He is a reporter. They sent him to the right place to find a flying saucer.

04:06.560 --> 04:08.560
Won't do no good, though.

04:08.560 --> 04:10.560
He opens his mouth about a flying saucer,

04:10.560 --> 04:13.560
the whole town shuts up, tighter than a clam.

04:13.560 --> 04:15.560
Right, Ginny?

04:15.560 --> 04:17.560
Yeah.

04:22.560 --> 04:24.560
How long you say you've been in town?

04:24.560 --> 04:26.560
I've been in town a couple hours.

04:26.560 --> 04:27.560
What paper are you from?

04:27.560 --> 04:29.560
I'm with the Daily Globe in New York.

04:29.560 --> 04:33.560
Say, we're interested in reports of flying saucers spotted around this area.

04:33.560 --> 04:34.560
Sorry, never saw any.

04:34.560 --> 04:36.560
Well, do you know anybody who has?

04:36.560 --> 04:38.560
Nobody. Sorry.

04:38.560 --> 04:41.560
Sorry. I've heard that 20 times in a few hours I've been here.

04:41.560 --> 04:44.560
Sorry, we can tell you anything else, but don't talk about flying saucers.

04:44.560 --> 04:45.560
Why?

04:45.560 --> 05:14.560
Say, why is everyone so scared to talk about something they claim they've never seen?

05:15.560 --> 05:44.560
I thought I'd help you on tax.

05:44.560 --> 05:45.560
How'd you get in here?

05:45.560 --> 05:46.560
The hairpin.

05:46.560 --> 05:48.560
Seen them doing it once in the pitches. Worked fine.

05:48.560 --> 05:49.560
Now listen, old man.

05:49.560 --> 05:51.560
Now take it easy. I'm kind of fragile.

05:51.560 --> 05:53.560
What were you looking for?

05:53.560 --> 05:56.560
Nothing particular. Just checking.

05:56.560 --> 05:59.560
There's a law against this kind of thing, or doesn't that interest you?

05:59.560 --> 06:01.560
Not particularly.

06:01.560 --> 06:06.560
You've been tipped on these flying saucers for quite a while, ain't you, Lieutenant?

06:06.560 --> 06:09.560
You did a thorough job, didn't you?

06:09.560 --> 06:12.560
Army grounded you for seeing too many flying saucers, didn't they?

06:12.560 --> 06:16.560
Army don't like their pilots seeing too many mirages like flying saucers.

06:16.560 --> 06:22.560
Next thing you know, you might bump into one of them saucers that ain't there and smash up a shiny new airplane.

06:22.560 --> 06:23.560
Keep talking.

06:23.560 --> 06:25.560
About through.

06:25.560 --> 06:27.560
Don't get mad, son. Had to check on you.

06:27.560 --> 06:30.560
Had to find out if he was on my side.

06:30.560 --> 06:36.560
Never can tell if it's one of them or one of us.

06:36.560 --> 06:38.560
One of whom?

06:38.560 --> 06:39.560
Them.

06:39.560 --> 06:42.560
Them. Them what comes in on the saucers.

06:42.560 --> 06:44.560
Then there are saucers here.

06:44.560 --> 06:47.560
They've been spotted here, man. They land them here. That's what they do.

06:47.560 --> 06:50.560
Over on the hill over there, they land them and they pull them into a cave.

06:50.560 --> 06:51.560
I've seen plenty.

06:51.560 --> 06:53.560
And the rest of the town?

06:53.560 --> 06:54.560
Can't tell.

06:54.560 --> 06:57.560
Some of them clam up because they're the ones that come in on the saucers.

06:57.560 --> 06:59.560
Well, the rest are just scared.

06:59.560 --> 07:02.560
They've seen him, but they're scared. Scared of the britches.

07:02.560 --> 07:04.560
Why? Why are they scared?

07:04.560 --> 07:08.560
Oh, they've got reasons. Real good reasons.

07:08.560 --> 07:11.560
Don't scare me. No more they don't scare me.

07:11.560 --> 07:12.560
I've got something to show you.

07:12.560 --> 07:14.560
You'll knock the ice plum out of your head.

07:14.560 --> 07:16.560
You wait right here. Don't you move. Don't you move.

07:24.560 --> 07:25.560
Oh, hi.

07:25.560 --> 07:26.560
Hi.

07:26.560 --> 07:27.560
Didn't see you come in.

07:27.560 --> 07:28.560
How'd you make out?

07:28.560 --> 07:29.560
Oh, big fat zero.

07:29.560 --> 07:33.560
Mention the saucers and this whole town runs a zipper right through their lips.

07:33.560 --> 07:34.560
They're sick of it, Vic.

07:34.560 --> 07:36.560
The publicity. It makes us all out to be fooled.

07:36.560 --> 07:38.560
That's too pat. They're not tired or resentful.

07:38.560 --> 07:39.560
They're just frightened.

07:39.560 --> 07:41.560
They're frightened of something they claim they've never seen.

07:41.560 --> 07:43.560
Claim? They haven't seen any flying saucers.

07:43.560 --> 07:45.560
Oh, but they have. I know they have.

07:45.560 --> 07:48.560
Vic, why are you so excited about this?

07:48.560 --> 07:50.560
I'm a newspaper man.

07:50.560 --> 07:52.560
Just a good story, that's all.

07:52.560 --> 07:55.560
Seems to me this is more than just a good story to you.

07:55.560 --> 07:58.560
Prove that flying saucers exist. Why?

07:58.560 --> 08:00.560
Because I know they exist.

08:00.560 --> 08:02.560
Okay.

08:02.560 --> 08:04.560
Wait a minute, Ginny.

08:04.560 --> 08:07.560
Look, just trust me, will you? Believe in me.

08:07.560 --> 08:09.560
You're right. It is more than just a story.

08:09.560 --> 08:11.560
Has to be.

08:11.560 --> 08:12.560
You're not a newspaper man.

08:12.560 --> 08:15.560
Hey, don't tell me you've been through my suitcase, too.

08:15.560 --> 08:17.560
Well, you haven't got a typewriter.

08:17.560 --> 08:20.560
I took a stab at that and little I know about you.

08:20.560 --> 08:21.560
I'm sorry.

08:21.560 --> 08:23.560
Oh, wait a minute. Don't get sore.

08:23.560 --> 08:25.560
I shouldn't have made that crack to me.

08:25.560 --> 08:27.560
Maybe I was sticking my nose where it doesn't belong.

08:27.560 --> 08:30.560
Hey, it's kind of a nice nose for nose.

08:30.560 --> 08:31.560
Hey.

08:31.560 --> 08:34.560
You said you were going to stay right there and not move, and you did.

08:34.560 --> 08:35.560
You moved.

08:35.560 --> 08:37.560
Can't trust nobody about nothing no more.

08:37.560 --> 08:39.560
Look, look what I brought you.

08:39.560 --> 08:40.560
Surprise you, don't it?

08:40.560 --> 08:42.560
It's a piece of one of their saucers.

08:42.560 --> 08:43.560
The Indian gave it to me.

08:43.560 --> 08:45.560
One of them blew up right near his place.

08:45.560 --> 08:47.560
Wasn't nothing left but smoke.

08:47.560 --> 08:49.560
And this.

08:49.560 --> 08:51.560
It's nothing but just a piece of steel.

08:51.560 --> 08:53.560
Steel?

08:53.560 --> 08:55.560
Man, that's what you're looking for.

08:55.560 --> 08:57.560
Is this your proof?

08:57.560 --> 08:58.560
Yes.

08:58.560 --> 08:59.560
You want to know?

08:59.560 --> 09:00.560
What it seemed with my own eyes.

09:00.560 --> 09:01.560
You're talking gibberish.

09:01.560 --> 09:02.560
See, she's scared.

09:02.560 --> 09:03.560
She's scared too.

09:03.560 --> 09:05.560
They're all scared they're going to end up like the Indian did.

09:05.560 --> 09:06.560
He's mad.

09:06.560 --> 09:07.560
Vic, you can't take his word against the Tom's.

09:07.560 --> 09:08.560
His word and mine.

09:08.560 --> 09:10.560
Two of us who swear we've seen flying saucers.

09:10.560 --> 09:12.560
What about the rest of the world?

09:12.560 --> 09:16.560
They'd believe it too if they know about the Indian and the reporter from Frisco.

09:16.560 --> 09:17.560
Made it look like an accident.

09:17.560 --> 09:18.560
Vic, he's mad.

09:18.560 --> 09:19.560
Everybody knows.

09:19.560 --> 09:20.560
Wait a minute, wait a minute.

09:20.560 --> 09:21.560
Let's hear him out.

09:21.560 --> 09:22.560
No.

09:22.560 --> 09:24.560
Not until you heard me out.

09:24.560 --> 09:28.560
Vic, you came here to talk to responsible people about the flying saucers.

09:28.560 --> 09:29.560
All right, I'll buy that.

09:29.560 --> 09:31.560
It's a worthy aim.

09:31.560 --> 09:33.560
But know something.

09:33.560 --> 09:37.560
Every flying saucer story that's come out of Los Palmas has come from one source.

09:37.560 --> 09:39.560
One source only.

09:39.560 --> 09:41.560
Hey, is that right?

09:41.560 --> 09:42.560
Right?

09:42.560 --> 09:43.560
Sure.

09:43.560 --> 09:44.560
Sure, it's right.

09:44.560 --> 09:45.560
I had to.

09:45.560 --> 09:46.560
Everybody else was scared.

09:46.560 --> 09:48.560
Guess I'm just about the town's only reporter.

09:48.560 --> 09:49.560
Only reporter.

09:49.560 --> 09:52.560
Wait a minute, I didn't tell you about the Indian and the reporter from Frisco.

09:52.560 --> 09:54.560
Leave him alone.

09:54.560 --> 09:56.560
Why?

09:56.560 --> 09:59.560
Getting sweet on him?

09:59.560 --> 10:01.560
Afraid he's going to get hurt?

10:01.560 --> 10:03.560
Leave him alone or get out of my house.

10:12.560 --> 10:13.560
I'm sorry.

10:13.560 --> 10:15.560
It's not your fault.

10:15.560 --> 10:19.560
I knew you were going to find out about the flying saucers story sooner or later.

10:19.560 --> 10:22.560
I didn't want you up on a cloud when it happened.

10:22.560 --> 10:23.560
Don't believe me either, do you?

10:23.560 --> 10:25.560
You think I'm crazy, huh?

10:25.560 --> 10:28.560
Crazy Vic and Crazy John, the flying saucer boys.

10:28.560 --> 10:29.560
Please, Vic, stop it.

10:29.560 --> 10:31.560
Now, wait a minute, Jenny, listen to me.

10:31.560 --> 10:35.560
All right, maybe I was taken in by a crazy old man who showed me a hunk of steel, okay.

10:35.560 --> 10:38.560
But, Jenny, I know what I saw, and I saw a flying saucer.

10:38.560 --> 10:40.560
Not just something way over yonder behind a hill.

10:40.560 --> 10:42.560
Not just a streak of light on a dark sky.

10:42.560 --> 10:46.560
Not just a creation of a sick mind that was molded into the shape of a saucer.

10:46.560 --> 10:47.560
I saw it, Jenny.

10:47.560 --> 10:49.560
It was close enough to reach out and touch.

10:49.560 --> 10:52.560
There it was at my wingtip, then back at my tail.

10:52.560 --> 10:53.560
Up and down, all around the plane.

10:53.560 --> 10:55.560
Jenny, this thing was trying to fight me.

10:55.560 --> 10:57.560
It was trying to destroy me.

10:57.560 --> 10:58.560
I can't be wrong about that.

10:58.560 --> 11:00.560
Nobody can tell me I'm wrong.

11:02.560 --> 11:03.560
All right, Vic.

11:04.560 --> 11:05.560
You said I'd leave you.

11:06.560 --> 11:08.560
I'd leave you, darling.

11:18.560 --> 11:20.560
I'd leave you, darling.

11:20.560 --> 11:22.560
I'd leave you, darling.

11:36.560 --> 11:38.560
And now, before we go on with our drama,

11:38.560 --> 11:42.560
let's take a look at another kind of drama from the world of yesterday.

11:42.560 --> 11:47.560
The days of dueling and the famous Count of Monte Cristo.

11:47.560 --> 11:51.560
Yes, men lived daringly, dramatically, in Monte Cristo's time.

11:51.560 --> 11:54.560
So now, Chrysler takes designs from Monte Cristo's times

11:54.560 --> 11:57.560
to bring you dramatic new ideas and watch bands.

11:57.560 --> 11:59.560
Here in Chrysler's unique pistol box,

11:59.560 --> 12:02.560
you see one of the fabulous Monte Cristo watch bands for men.

12:02.560 --> 12:08.560
This rich-looking jewel crest is just one of the new styles in Chrysler's Monte Cristo bands.

12:09.560 --> 12:12.560
Yes, it brings a costly jewelry look to your watch.

12:12.560 --> 12:15.560
And imagine what this brilliant band will do.

12:15.560 --> 12:18.560
Not only for your watch, but for your appearance.

12:18.560 --> 12:23.560
This magnificent jewel crest is typical of Chrysler craftsmanship.

12:24.560 --> 12:28.560
Here is another Monte Cristo design, using a royal crown.

12:28.560 --> 12:34.560
Best of all, Chrysler's Monte Cristo watch bands cost no more than many ordinary bands.

12:34.560 --> 12:37.560
Only $12.95, tax included.

12:37.560 --> 12:41.560
And all Chrysler bands have super-calibrated seven-coil springs

12:41.560 --> 12:44.560
for extra expansion, extra flexibility.

12:44.560 --> 12:49.560
So buy the Chrysler Monte Cristo at your jeweler.

12:49.560 --> 12:54.560
Make your watch look better than new with Chrysler.

12:54.560 --> 13:11.560
And now, for the last act of The Search for the Flying Saucer, starring Jack Cotter.

13:11.560 --> 13:14.560
It's open.

13:14.560 --> 13:18.560
I thought you might still be up.

13:18.560 --> 13:24.560
Would you go for some coffee?

13:24.560 --> 13:26.560
Yeah, I guess I would.

13:26.560 --> 13:28.560
I'll do better than that. I'll get it for you.

13:28.560 --> 13:30.560
Ginny. Ginny, darling.

13:30.560 --> 13:34.560
Vic. Vic, why must this be this way?

13:34.560 --> 13:36.560
You said you loved me.

13:36.560 --> 13:38.560
You came into my life and turned everything upside down.

13:38.560 --> 13:40.560
And now you're going away. Why?

13:40.560 --> 13:42.560
Honey, I try to explain all that.

13:42.560 --> 13:45.560
Three hours ago, you held me in your arms and said you felt as if you'd found me.

13:45.560 --> 13:49.560
Three hours ago, you held me in your arms and said you felt as if you'd found a home.

13:49.560 --> 13:53.560
You said you loved me and then said not to get involved, that you were leaving in the morning.

13:53.560 --> 13:55.560
I think I've a right to ask why.

13:55.560 --> 13:57.560
Honey, you've got every right to know.

13:57.560 --> 13:59.560
It's about time you knew something about me.

13:59.560 --> 14:02.560
I know enough. I know I love you.

14:02.560 --> 14:04.560
Here, wait a minute.

14:04.560 --> 14:06.560
Here, look at this.

14:06.560 --> 14:08.560
Recognize that?

14:08.560 --> 14:10.560
Army flight cap.

14:10.560 --> 14:13.560
Special War Department orders. Reassignment.

14:13.560 --> 14:16.560
What that really means is they figure any guy who's seen too many flying saucers

14:16.560 --> 14:18.560
maybe is a little air happy.

14:18.560 --> 14:20.560
So they figure the best thing is to keep him on the ground.

14:20.560 --> 14:22.560
OK, you were grounded by the Air Corps. Big deal.

14:22.560 --> 14:24.560
Big deal it is to me.

14:24.560 --> 14:26.560
Flying's my whole life and they won't let me fly.

14:26.560 --> 14:28.560
They will, Vic. Honey, tell them it was all a mistake.

14:28.560 --> 14:30.560
Oh, I can't. I can't.

14:30.560 --> 14:32.560
What can't you do? Tell me. I'd really like to know.

14:32.560 --> 14:35.560
I can prove that the saucers exist. That's what I can do.

14:35.560 --> 14:37.560
Vic, how can you find saucers when there are none?

14:37.560 --> 14:40.560
But there are. There are saucers. There are hundreds of them.

14:40.560 --> 14:43.560
Look at this. Flying saucer stories.

14:43.560 --> 14:45.560
Spreading from coast to coast over the last four years.

14:45.560 --> 14:48.560
Here. Doctors, scientists, chemists, housewives.

14:48.560 --> 14:51.560
Here, look at this. A dozen kids in Spokane, Washington.

14:51.560 --> 14:54.560
Sure we saw the saucers. A dozen of them flying in formation.

14:54.560 --> 14:57.560
Here, look at this. A restaurant owner in Mojave, California.

14:57.560 --> 15:01.560
Don't tell me it was a jet. This thing moved faster than any jet I ever saw.

15:01.560 --> 15:03.560
A minister in Boston. Here.

15:03.560 --> 15:05.560
Four fishermen in San Francisco.

15:05.560 --> 15:07.560
And seven reports from right here in Las Palmas.

15:07.560 --> 15:10.560
I tell you, they've all seen saucers. They've all swore they've seen them.

15:10.560 --> 15:12.560
And, Ginny, I swear it, too.

15:18.560 --> 15:20.560
I mentioned something about coffee.

15:20.560 --> 15:38.560
I did want to change your mind.

15:38.560 --> 15:41.560
Hey, you going someplace?

15:41.560 --> 15:44.560
I told you you'd say I was crazy.

15:44.560 --> 15:47.560
Would you like to catch a glimpse of a flying saucer?

15:47.560 --> 15:48.560
Flying saucer?

15:48.560 --> 15:51.560
Flying saucer. You mean another one of your pieces of steel.

15:51.560 --> 15:52.560
Don't forget that.

15:52.560 --> 15:55.560
I know you wouldn't have good enough sense to appreciate that.

15:55.560 --> 15:56.560
Nobody does but me and the Indian.

15:56.560 --> 15:58.560
He's seen them, the Indian did.

15:58.560 --> 16:00.560
He chased one halfway across the field and up a cliff.

16:00.560 --> 16:02.560
And they found him killed.

16:02.560 --> 16:04.560
Can I make a trek with me?

16:04.560 --> 16:06.560
They landed another one tonight.

16:06.560 --> 16:09.560
Can't see it from here, but if we was to go up by the Indian shack and talk to Maria,

16:09.560 --> 16:11.560
that's it, we could talk to Maria.

16:11.560 --> 16:13.560
Well, wait a minute. Wait a minute. Let me tell Ginny.

16:13.560 --> 16:15.560
No, no, no, no. She'll just talk you out of it like she did before.

16:15.560 --> 16:16.560
Come in and sit.

16:16.560 --> 16:18.560
Okay, let's go.

16:18.560 --> 16:47.560
Wait.

16:47.560 --> 16:48.560
Alone?

16:48.560 --> 16:51.560
No, the old man.

16:51.560 --> 16:54.560
I think you'd better come right over here.

16:59.560 --> 17:02.560
This man ain't getting tired.

17:02.560 --> 17:04.560
This here's Jack.

17:04.560 --> 17:07.560
Maria's my niece. She's nine on eight years old.

17:07.560 --> 17:09.560
See that hill over there?

17:09.560 --> 17:13.560
That's where they land the saucers and pull them back into a cave.

17:13.560 --> 17:15.560
No, can't see nothing.

17:15.560 --> 17:17.560
Must have pulled it back into a cave already.

17:17.560 --> 17:19.560
Maria, you got something?

17:25.560 --> 17:27.560
Maria?

17:27.560 --> 17:34.560
Maria?

17:39.560 --> 17:42.560
Maria?

17:42.560 --> 17:44.560
Come and get it.

17:44.560 --> 17:48.560
First time here, and a reporter from Frisco.

17:48.560 --> 17:50.560
Now Maria.

17:50.560 --> 17:53.560
All of us who've seen the saucers, you come and get us.

17:53.560 --> 17:54.560
One by one.

17:54.560 --> 17:59.560
Now listen, old man, this is twice in one night you threw a sucker pitch and I went fighting for it.

17:59.560 --> 18:01.560
Now what's the idea of dragging me up here? There's nobody lived here.

18:01.560 --> 18:04.560
There's nobody lived in this shack for years.

18:11.560 --> 18:12.560
Them ashes.

18:12.560 --> 18:15.560
They ain't going a couple hours cold, wouldn't you say?

18:15.560 --> 18:20.560
I warned Maria it was dangerous living here alone so close to that cave.

18:20.560 --> 18:24.560
She never existed except in your twisted mind.

18:25.560 --> 18:27.560
Twisted mind, huh?

18:27.560 --> 18:29.560
Is that what the army said about you?

18:29.560 --> 18:32.560
Now look, the Indian know this whole area like a man knows his own backyard.

18:32.560 --> 18:36.560
Ain't it kind of strange he should all of a sudden fall off the cliff?

18:36.560 --> 18:39.560
Strange no one believes that story but you.

18:39.560 --> 18:41.560
I didn't believe it neither.

18:41.560 --> 18:43.560
I got me some groceries and I made me a trek up there.

18:43.560 --> 18:45.560
Oh, now wait a minute.

18:45.560 --> 18:47.560
You got some groceries and you made a trek up here?

18:47.560 --> 18:49.560
With no help at all? With that leg?

18:49.560 --> 18:50.560
Now listen old man.

18:50.560 --> 18:54.560
I wasn't born with this bad leg.

18:54.560 --> 19:23.560
Fourteen months ago, somebody shoved me off on a cliff.

19:23.560 --> 19:24.560
You're with me?

19:24.560 --> 19:26.560
Hi Jenny.

19:26.560 --> 19:28.560
Hey, it's five AM. Why aren't you asleep?

19:37.560 --> 19:39.560
You going back there?

19:39.560 --> 19:40.560
Back where?

19:40.560 --> 19:41.560
How do you know where he took me?

19:41.560 --> 19:44.560
Oh Vic, stop it. Please stop it. You sound just like him.

19:44.560 --> 19:48.560
You think that old idiot ever blabbered about anything but that Indian and his wife and...

19:48.560 --> 19:49.560
Then there was an Indian.

19:49.560 --> 19:50.560
An old man.

19:50.560 --> 19:54.560
An old man who died six months ago of a heart attack or old age.

19:54.560 --> 19:56.560
At the foot of a cliff at two o'clock in the morning?

19:56.560 --> 19:58.560
That's a crazy John story.

19:58.560 --> 20:00.560
Yeah, well maybe but it won't hurt to look into it.

20:00.560 --> 20:04.560
Anyhow, we're going to get some grub and some horses and we'll go up there and find out.

20:04.560 --> 20:06.560
Then if there's a cave, if there's concrete evidence, great.

20:06.560 --> 20:09.560
If not, I'll just have to start and look somewhere else.

20:09.560 --> 20:13.560
Vic, please. For me. Please don't go with him.

20:13.560 --> 20:14.560
Why? Why not?

20:14.560 --> 20:17.560
I don't want you to. It might be dangerous.

20:17.560 --> 20:21.560
Only madmen see the sauces but they're dangerous.

20:21.560 --> 20:23.560
There's nothing out in those hills but the hills are dangerous.

20:23.560 --> 20:24.560
Yes.

20:24.560 --> 20:26.560
I can't buy that.

20:26.560 --> 20:29.560
The only way I can hurt myself is by not seeing this thing through.

20:29.560 --> 20:32.560
Any other way I could never live with myself.

20:32.560 --> 20:35.560
Jenny, I've got to find out.

20:35.560 --> 20:37.560
Then...

20:37.560 --> 20:39.560
Then I can't stop you.

20:39.560 --> 20:41.560
No, you can't.

20:41.560 --> 20:44.560
But I'll be back, Jenny, and so help me if this long shot comes through.

20:44.560 --> 20:50.560
If John's story is right, it'll mean a new world for me, for you, for the both of us.

20:50.560 --> 20:54.560
If I can get some proof, some concrete evidence, some real documents

20:54.560 --> 20:56.560
that these sauces have been landing.

20:56.560 --> 20:58.560
I know they have. They've been landing here for the last three years.

20:58.560 --> 21:00.560
Landing and discharging passengers. And where are they?

21:00.560 --> 21:03.560
Here in Las Palmas, filtered all over America.

21:03.560 --> 21:05.560
You really believe that, don't you?

21:05.560 --> 21:07.560
I don't know, Jenny, but I'll find out. I'll find out a lot of things.

21:07.560 --> 21:09.560
I'll find out why they come here.

21:09.560 --> 21:11.560
Who sent them? What's behind all this?

21:11.560 --> 21:13.560
Who wants them here?

21:13.560 --> 21:15.560
Jenny, I'll find out a lot of things.

21:15.560 --> 21:17.560
Yes, yes, you will.

21:26.560 --> 21:28.560
Jenny.

21:32.560 --> 21:34.560
You know something?

21:34.560 --> 21:44.560
I could really fall in love with a girl like you.

21:55.560 --> 21:57.560
Well?

21:57.560 --> 21:59.560
I couldn't stop him. He's beaten us.

21:59.560 --> 22:01.560
Nonsense.

22:01.560 --> 22:03.560
It would be a subtle matter to dispose of him.

22:03.560 --> 22:04.560
What about the others?

22:04.560 --> 22:05.560
What others?

22:05.560 --> 22:07.560
You don't understand. He's not alone.

22:07.560 --> 22:08.560
There are hundreds of them, thousands of them.

22:08.560 --> 22:09.560
You're mad.

22:09.560 --> 22:11.560
I'm mad enough to save us.

22:11.560 --> 22:14.560
Mad enough to see what you and I and the rest of our planet couldn't see.

22:14.560 --> 22:16.560
We underestimated them.

22:16.560 --> 22:18.560
Their world is full of Icarus souls.

22:18.560 --> 22:22.560
Full of fools who chase dreams over dark mountains and discover the truth.

22:22.560 --> 22:25.560
Kill one and another takes his place.

22:25.560 --> 22:27.560
We didn't understand them.

22:27.560 --> 22:29.560
We didn't realize their strength and their courage.

22:29.560 --> 22:30.560
You're trying to save them.

22:30.560 --> 22:32.560
I'm trying to save us.

22:32.560 --> 22:34.560
He's a flittering idiot, but we're wrong.

22:34.560 --> 22:36.560
He knows. He knows the truth.

22:36.560 --> 22:37.560
He knows nothing.

22:37.560 --> 22:38.560
You're wrong.

22:38.560 --> 22:40.560
A piece of metal the old man gave him.

22:40.560 --> 22:41.560
You said he'd thrown it away.

22:41.560 --> 22:44.560
He sent it to his commanding officer with a report.

22:44.560 --> 22:50.560
If he's found dead, if he disappears, they'll believe his story.

22:50.560 --> 22:52.560
This place will crawl with soldiers.

22:52.560 --> 22:54.560
You're lying.

22:54.560 --> 22:55.560
I warn you.

22:55.560 --> 22:57.560
If you're lying to save him, if you've fallen in love with him, I...

22:57.560 --> 23:00.560
They didn't send us here to fall in love.

23:00.560 --> 23:06.560
They're here to tell the truth.

23:06.560 --> 23:14.560
Get ready to leave. We're pulling out.

23:14.560 --> 23:16.560
Must I go with you?

23:16.560 --> 23:18.560
Did you ask?

23:18.560 --> 23:47.560
We're leaving in half an hour.

23:47.560 --> 24:03.560
Darling.

24:03.560 --> 24:07.560
They didn't send me here to fall in love.

24:07.560 --> 24:25.560
They wouldn't let me stay.

24:25.560 --> 24:28.560
So ends our tale of tomorrow.

24:28.560 --> 24:31.560
Two weeks from tonight, look for Enemy Unknown,

24:31.560 --> 24:37.560
starring Ron McAllister, Walter Abel, and Edith Fellows.

24:37.560 --> 24:43.560
And now a final word from the Chrysler kids.

24:43.560 --> 24:45.560
Jack Chrysler. Jack Chrysler.

24:45.560 --> 24:47.560
On my hand. On your hand.

24:47.560 --> 24:50.560
Brown with royal crest to make it look grand.

24:50.560 --> 24:53.560
So grand. It's Chrysler. It's Chrysler.

24:53.560 --> 24:55.560
For me too. For you too.

24:55.560 --> 24:57.560
Makes your watch look better than new.

24:57.560 --> 24:59.560
Quality, style, and value too.

24:59.560 --> 25:03.560
It's the man they'll all have. It's Chrysler.

25:03.560 --> 25:05.560
Makes your watch look at its best.

25:05.560 --> 25:34.560
The glamour of a royal crest. It's Chrysler.

25:34.560 --> 26:02.560
Ah.

26:02.560 --> 26:19.560
Dressing stresses by Tina Lesser.

26:19.560 --> 26:21.560
Men, make your watch look better than new

26:21.560 --> 26:24.560
with Chrysler's handsome Monte Cristo expansion watch band

26:24.560 --> 26:27.560
with a richly enameled crest.

26:27.560 --> 26:30.560
And ladies, see how Chrysler's new flirtation watch band

26:30.560 --> 26:32.560
adds jeweled beauty to your watch.

26:32.560 --> 26:36.560
Remember, look for Jack Chrysler at your jeweler.

26:36.560 --> 26:42.560
It's the name that means quality in watch bands.

26:42.560 --> 26:45.560
Join us two weeks from tonight when Chrysler brings you

26:45.560 --> 26:49.560
Lon McAllister and Walter Abel in Enemy Unknown.

26:49.560 --> 26:55.560
A new adventure in Tales of Tomorrow.

26:55.560 --> 26:59.560
The preceding program, originally telecast by ABC in New York,

26:59.560 --> 27:01.560
is brought to you by Special Video Recording.

27:01.560 --> 27:29.560
This is ABC, the American broadcasting company.

